# Triage Factory — multi-mode environment template.
#
# `triagefactory deploy init` writes .env from this file with every required
# value filled in (docs/users/self-hosting/install.md). By hand:
#
#   cp .env.example .env               # then fill in the REQUIRED section
#   ./triagefactory jwk-init --write-env .env
#   docker compose up -d
#
# (docs/contributors/multi-mode-by-hand.md). A blank required value fails
# `docker compose up`. Commented lines (# VAR=default) are optional and show
# their default.
#
# Generate keys and passwords with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Use hex, not
# base64: some values are written into database URLs, where `/ + =` break
# parsing.
#
# Any secret can instead come from a mounted file: set <NAME>_FILE to its path
# and leave <NAME> blank. Which ones the bundled compose forwards:
# docs/users/self-hosting/secrets.md.

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REQUIRED
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Postgres role passwords ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# One password per role, so a leak of one doesn't expose the others. Sidecars
# reapply them on every `docker compose up`, so you can rotate them here.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD is read only when the volume is first created; to change it
# later, also run ALTER USER postgres inside the postgres container.
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD             superuser (migrations, admin)
#   SUPABASE_AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD  GoTrue's role
#   TF_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD     the role control serves requests as
#   TF_SYSTEM_PASSWORD            the least-privilege role executors use
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
SUPABASE_AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
TF_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD=
TF_SYSTEM_PASSWORD=

# Public URL people reach this deployment at. No trailing slash. Set it before
# running jwk-init.
TF_PUBLIC_URL=

# ── GitHub OAuth app (sign-in; install step 2) ───────────────────────────
# Create on the deployment's GitHub, which sign-in uses:
# https://github.com/settings/developers, or <TF_DEFAULT_GITHUB_HOST>/settings/developers
# on a GitHub Enterprise Server. Callback ${TF_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/v1/callback.
# With a deployment GitHub App, these can be the App's Client ID and client
# secret instead (deployment-github-app.md, "sign people in through the App").
GH_CLIENT_ID=
GH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── At-rest encryption keys ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three separate keys, so each can be rotated on its own:
#   TF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY  sign-in sessions. Changing it signs everyone out.
#   TF_COOKIE_SECRET           the sign-in state cookie. Changing it only breaks
#                              sign-ins in progress.
#   TF_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY   every stored integration credential. Changing or
#                              losing it means re-entering all of them.
TF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
TF_COOKIE_SECRET=
TF_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── Object store ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two buckets on one S3-compatible endpoint (the bundled SeaweedFS, S3, R2 or
# Supabase Storage):
#   tf-workspaces  workspace snapshots, so a run's workspace outlives its
#                  executor. A cache: overwritten at every checkpoint and
#                  cleaned up by Triage Factory. Executors only.
#   tf-knowledge   team knowledge-base documents, the only copy of what people
#                  wrote. Versioned in the bundled stack. Control writes it;
#                  executors read it.
# Each bucket has its own key pair, and executors get the knowledge bucket's
# read-only pair, so a compromised executor cannot rewrite the knowledge every
# later run starts with. Multi mode refuses to boot without TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT,
# and an executor refuses both buckets having the same name.
#
# With the bundled SeaweedFS, fill in the eight keys below. The stack turns
# them into SeaweedFS identities, each limited to its bucket, and gives each
# service only its own pairs: control gets TF_KNOWLEDGE_* and no workspace
# pair; an executor gets TF_WORKSPACE_* and, as its TF_KNOWLEDGE_*, the
# TF_KNOWLEDGE_READER_* pair. Only the seaweedfs-postinit sidecar holds the
# SEAWEEDFS_ADMIN_* pair; it creates both buckets and turns versioning on for
# tf-knowledge. Generate each with `openssl rand -hex 32` (an access key needs
# at least 3 characters and a secret at least 8; 64 hex characters satisfies
# both). The four access keys must all differ. `triagefactory deploy init`
# generates them for you.
TF_WORKSPACE_ACCESS_KEY=
TF_WORKSPACE_SECRET_KEY=
TF_KNOWLEDGE_ACCESS_KEY=
TF_KNOWLEDGE_SECRET_KEY=
TF_KNOWLEDGE_READER_ACCESS_KEY=
TF_KNOWLEDGE_READER_SECRET_KEY=
SEAWEEDFS_ADMIN_ACCESS_KEY=
SEAWEEDFS_ADMIN_SECRET_KEY=
#
# To use S3, R2 or Supabase Storage instead, set the pairs to that backend's
# keys (scoped per role; see docs/users/self-hosting/storage.md), pre-create
# the buckets and uncomment the overrides. The seaweedfs service still starts
# but goes unused; if you remove it in an override, also drop
# seaweedfs-postinit from control's and the executor's depends_on. With an
# instance role instead of keys, the binary takes neither key of a pair and
# uses the AWS default credential chain; the bundled compose requires every
# key, so that is a custom deployment.
#   TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT     scheme://host[:port][/base/path]. The scheme selects
#                        TLS and the base path is kept (S3/R2 https://host,
#                        Supabase https://<ref>.supabase.co/storage/v1/s3).
#                        Default http://seaweedfs:8333
#   TF_BLOB_REGION       S3 region (S3 and Supabase want it, SeaweedFS ignores
#                        it). Default us-east-1
#   TF_WORKSPACE_BUCKET  workspace-snapshot bucket. Default tf-workspaces
#   TF_KNOWLEDGE_BUCKET  knowledge-base bucket, with versioning. Default tf-knowledge
# TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT=
# TF_BLOB_REGION=
# TF_WORKSPACE_BUCKET=
# TF_KNOWLEDGE_BUCKET=

# ── Sign-in signing key (written by jwk-init) ────────────────────────────
# `./triagefactory jwk-init --write-env .env` writes GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS,
# GOTRUE_JWT_SECRET and TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN. Don't set them by hand.
# Re-running keeps the existing key; --rotate replaces it and signs everyone
# out (docs/users/self-hosting/jwt-key-rotation.md).
# TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN=

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OPTIONAL, DEPLOYMENT-WIDE
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# GitHub Enterprise Server only: your GHES web URL. It is also the GitHub
# people sign in with, so the OAuth app above must be registered there. Set it
# BEFORE the first organization is created; records are keyed under this host
# and never migrated, so changing it later means a fresh install. Leave it
# unset for github.com: multi mode refuses to boot with it set to github.com.
# TF_DEFAULT_GITHUB_HOST=https://ghe.example.com

# true makes the deployment invite-only (no self-service organization
# creation). Default false.
# TF_PREVENT_ORG_CREATION=false

# ── Enterprise license ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Signed token from your vendor; unlocks SSO, Slack, governance and the fleet
# console. Blank leaves them off. control logs
# `Enterprise license: <org> (features: [...])` on success.
TF_LICENSE=
# Development and fork builds only: a different license verification key,
# passed to the image build by compose.dev.yml, so it applies only when building
# from source (see the end of this file). Leave unset to verify official
# licenses. Using it to circumvent license key functionality in production
# violates the license.
# TF_LICENSE_PUBLIC_KEY=

# ── Deployment GitHub App ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One App every organization without its own can connect through. Set all
# four or none. Mount the private key as a file (TF_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE).
# When sign-in runs through this App, GH_CLIENT_SECRET holds the same client
# secret: rotate the two together.
# docs/users/self-hosting/deployment-integrations/deployment-github-app.md
# TF_GITHUB_APP_ID=
# TF_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# TF_GITHUB_APP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# TF_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Deployment commit signer ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The GitHub account organizations on the deployment App sign their commits as.
# Login and key together or neither. Email required off github.com.
# docs/users/self-hosting/deployment-integrations/deployment-commit-signer.md
# TF_GITHUB_COMMIT_SIGNER_LOGIN=
# TF_GITHUB_COMMIT_SIGNER_KEY_FILE=
# TF_GITHUB_COMMIT_SIGNER_EMAIL=

# ── Deployment Jira app (Atlassian OAuth) ────────────────────────────────
# One-click Connect Jira for organizations on Jira Cloud without their own app.
# docs/users/self-hosting/deployment-integrations/deployment-jira-app.md
# TF_ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_ID=
# TF_ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Deployment Linear app ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One-click Install Triage Factory for organizations without their own Linear
# app. Set both or neither.
# docs/users/self-hosting/deployment-integrations/deployment-linear-app.md
# TF_LINEAR_CLIENT_ID=
# TF_LINEAR_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── SAML SSO ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off by default. To enable, uncomment GOTRUE_SAML_ENABLED and set
# GOTRUE_SAML_PRIVATE_KEY (an RSA key, not the sign-in key above; keep it
# stable). Each organization then configures its identity provider in the app.
# Generate the key once:
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm rsa -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -outform DER | base64 -w0
# docs/users/self-hosting/sso.md
# GOTRUE_SAML_ENABLED=true
GOTRUE_SAML_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ── Bedrock role mode ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only if organizations authenticate to Bedrock by assuming an IAM role; the
# control service then needs an AWS identity of its own.
# docs/users/self-hosting/bedrock-role-mode.md
# Lifetime of the minted credentials, in seconds. Default 3600; minimum 900.
# TF_LLM_CRED_TTL_SEC=3600
# Restrict credentials handed to executors to their egress (comma-separated).
# TF_EXECUTOR_EGRESS_CIDRS=10.0.0.0/8
# TF_EXECUTOR_VPCE_IDS=vpce-0abc123,vpce-0def456

# ── Client IP behind a proxy ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Behind a load balancer or CDN, list its egress CIDRs (and strip inbound
# X-Forwarded-For at the edge), or every request is recorded as the proxy's
# IP. Leave unset when TF is exposed directly.
# docs/users/self-hosting/networking.md
# TF_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR=10.0.0.0/8,192.168.0.0/16
# false stores no client IPs at all, and rate-limits all clients as one.
# TF_CAPTURE_CLIENT_IP=true

# ── Traces ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OTLP/HTTP endpoint for traces. The default is the bundled Tempo (Grafana on
# http://localhost:3030). Set another backend, or `off` (also required if you
# remove the tempo service). Keep the collector on a private network.
# docs/users/self-hosting/monitoring.md
# TF_TRACES_ENDPOINT=http://tempo:4318

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TUNING — the defaults suit most deployments
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The bundled compose sets TF_ROLE (control or executor) per service; set it
# yourself only in a custom deployment. Add executors with
# `triagefactory deploy up --executors N`, or by hand
# `docker compose up -d --scale executor=N` (docs/users/self-hosting/scaling.md).

# Executor health port: 127.0.0.1:<port>/healthz.
# TF_HEALTHZ_PORT=3001

# ── Run capacity (executors) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Concurrent agent runs per executor. Plan on about 0.25 GB of RAM per run plus
# a reserve (docs/contributors/benchmarks/sandbox-bench.md). Maximum 256.
# TF_MAX_CONCURRENT_CLAIMS=8
# Stop starting runs while free memory (MiB) is below this. 0 disables.
# TF_DISPATCH_MEM_FLOOR_MB=4096
# Stop starting runs, and evict parked workspaces, while free disk (MiB) is
# below this. 0 disables.
# TF_DISPATCH_DISK_FLOOR_MB=10240
# Memory ceiling per run (MiB); a run that exceeds it is killed alone. 0
# disables.
# TF_CLAIM_MEMORY_LIMIT_MB=4096
# Concurrent GitHub/Jira API calls one run may have in flight. <=0 disables.
# TF_AGENTHOST_MAX_UPSTREAM_CONCURRENCY=8
# How many times in a row a run may lose its executor before it is failed
# instead of retried.
# TF_MAX_CLAIM_ATTEMPTS=2

# ── Workspaces ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Seconds a parked workspace stays on an executor's disk before it is evicted
# (its stored snapshot is kept). 0 disables.
# TF_WORKSPACE_EVICT_AFTER_SEC=21600
# Seconds a resume waits for a snapshot another executor is still writing.
# TF_SNAPSHOT_WAIT_SEC=60
# Seconds between checkpoints of a running workspace; bounds the work lost if
# an executor dies. 0 disables.
# TF_SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_SEC=300

# ── Placement ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Prefer the executor that already holds a repository's clone. `off` disables.
# TF_PLACEMENT=on
# Seconds a queued run waits for that executor before any executor may take it.
# TF_PLACEMENT_AGING_SEC=20
# Seconds without a heartbeat after which that executor is treated as gone.
# TF_PLACEMENT_LIVENESS_SEC=12

# ── Run control ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How long control waits for an executor to acknowledge an interrupt or steer.
# TF_SIGNAL_ACK_TIMEOUT=5s
# How long acknowledged run signals are kept.
# TF_CONVERSATION_SIGNAL_PURGE_AFTER=24h
# How long a run an event started may keep working after another event
# resolves its task. Past it, TF stops the run. Runs a person started or wrote
# to are not stopped. Boot refuses a value that is not a positive duration.
# TF_CLOSE_HOLD_TIMEOUT=20m

# ── Commit signing limits (control) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Signatures per organization per minute, and per run in total.
# TF_MAX_COMMIT_SIGNATURES_PER_ORG_MINUTE=600
# TF_MAX_COMMIT_SIGNATURES_PER_CLAIM=5000

# ── Leader election (control) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# One control pod runs background work at a time. These defaults fail over in
# about 30 seconds; boot refuses combinations that could overlap.
# docs/users/self-hosting/scaling.md
# TF_LEASE_RENEW_SEC=5
# TF_LEASE_TTL_SEC=20
# TF_LEASE_DEMOTE_SEC=15

# ── Database connections ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Connections per pool (each pod opens two). Unset: 25 on control, 6 on
# executors. Raise Postgres max_connections to match (docs/users/self-hosting/scaling.md).
# TF_DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=
# Memory (MiB) reserved for other services, used only in the boot capacity log.
# TF_PLATFORM_RESERVE_MB=
# A direct (non-pooled) Postgres URL for LISTEN connections. Set it only when a
# transaction-mode pooler fronts the main database URL.
# TF_DATABASE_DIRECT_URL=

# ── Cross-pod websocket delivery (control) ───────────────────────────────
# Sizes and intervals for delivering live updates across control pods.
# TF_WS_INLINE_MAX_B=6144
# TF_WS_OUTBOX_TTL_SECONDS=60
# TF_WS_PRESENCE_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=15
# TF_WS_PRESENCE_TTL_SECONDS=45
# TF_WS_PRESENCE_ROW_TTL_SECONDS=300
# TF_WS_SESSION_REVALIDATE_SECONDS=60

# ── Executor process identity ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Match the image's built-in user; change only for a custom base image. The
# uid/gid must be outside 20000-20255 and 39999-40255.
# docs/users/security/privilege-separation.md
# TF_ORCHESTRATOR_UID=10001
# TF_ORCHESTRATOR_GID=10001
# TF_ORCHESTRATOR_HOME=/home/tf-orchestrator
# TF_CAPBROKER_SOCKET=/run/tf/cap-broker.sock

# ── Building from a source checkout (contributors only) ──────────────────
# docker-compose.yml runs the release image it pins. In a source checkout,
# uncomment this to layer compose.dev.yml over it, which builds the working
# tree as triagefactory:dev; `docker compose up -d --build` then runs that
# build. A deployment leaves it commented.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:compose.dev.yml
